  "text": " complex polynomial $q(x)$, also known as a cubic polynomial, it ensures that for there is a divisor polynomial making the difference of squares divisible by the product of linear factors.\nWe will then create the cubic polynomial $q(x)$ using the fact that it is divisible by the polynomial $\\left ( x^2 - x^6 \\right) = x\\cdot\\left ( x-\\text {anything something}\\right) .$ Since $y = x^6$ is an even function, in principle, $y^{6/7} -x^{6/3} = (x^2-x^3) = (x^6)^{1/6} - (x^3)^{1/3} = (x^6- 1)^{1/7} - (2x^3 - 1)^{1/3} = \\frac{y-1}{\\binom72y^5 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\binom63y^4 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\binom57y^3 + \\binom65y^2 + \\sqrt126y + 24} \\to x^6 - x / 2 = x(x^5 - 1/2),$ as we take the limit of this rational function restricted to the real $x > 0$ by first taking the large $y\\to\\infty$ and then taking the $\\infty\\to\\infty$ limit; the error term $1/y^9$ goes to 0 faster than $x^{-9}.$ $\\$ $\\$\nminecraftWordpress\nushmanmiss / june 10 2020",
